01 | The Evidence Base
Purpose: Explain where the inspection records came from and what the analysis considers an adverse outcome.

File in this section
DATA INSPECT-1.xlsx — cleaned Montgomery County inspection records, ready for Excel or Tableau.
Source and coverage
Publisher: Montgomery County, Maryland
Dataset: Food Inspection on Data.gov
Period reported by the team: October 5, 2022 – October 3, 2024
Fields: Establishment attributes, inspection outcome and type, date, and compliance checks.
How to use
Download DATA INSPECT-1.xlsx, open the DATA INSPECT worksheet, and filter on Inspection Results, Category, Inspection Type, or individual safety checks. For Tableau, reconnect the workbook to this file if asked.

Cleaning and interpretation
The team defined adverse outcomes as Critical Violations Corrected or Facility Closed, with No Critical Violations Noted as the comparison class. This is a project-defined label, not a government-issued pass/fail grade.

Record-count note: The team's written report states 11,854 inspections, while the supplied cleaned workbook has 11,835 data rows (plus a header). Confirm which cleaned version is authoritative before reporting an exact sample size.
